# The 64 message-schedule words, as circuit wires

`wordBits k` is the 32 input nodes carrying `W k`, least significant bit first — `input_block k`
for `k < 16` and `outw (k-16)` after. It exists so the 48 schedule steps *chain*: step `t` speaks
about `wordBits t`, `wordBits (t-2)`, `wordBits (t-7)`, `wordBits (t-15)` and `wordBits (t-16)`,
and step `t'` sees literally the same list, not a per-instance copy of it.

`bitsOf` reads those wires as `Nat` bits. `SHA256F.natBit` is noncomputable, so this is too; that
costs nothing, since the value is only ever compared, never run.
-/

